🔹 First, What’s the Purpose?
AWS CloudFormation → helps you define your infrastructure as code (YAML/JSON templates). Example: create an EC2 instance, S3 bucket, or VPC.

AWS CodePipeline → automates your deployments. Example: if a developer pushes new code or infra template to GitHub, the pipeline automatically deploys it using CloudFormation.

👉 Together: You can automate infrastructure deployments. Instead of manually creating EC2/S3 from the console, you push a CloudFormation template to GitHub, and CodePipeline applies it.

🔹 Real-Time Example:
Let’s say your team wants to automatically create an S3 bucket and an EC2 instance whenever the template is updated in GitHub.

Step 1️⃣: Create a CloudFormation Template
Create a file named infra.yaml:

AWSTemplateFormatVersion: '2010-09-09'
Description: Simple CloudFormation stack to create S3 bucket and EC2

Resources:
  MyBucket:
    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket
    Properties:
      BucketName: my-devops-demo-bucket-12345

  MyEC2Instance:
    Type: AWS::EC2::Instance
    Properties:
      InstanceType: t2.micro
      ImageId: ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0   # Amazon Linux 2 AMI ID (update for your region)
      Tags:
        - Key: Name
          Value: DevOpsDemoEC2
✅ Save and push this file to your GitHub repo.

Step 2️⃣: Create an S3 Bucket for CodePipeline Artifacts
CodePipeline needs a bucket to store packaged files.

aws s3 mb s3://my-devops-artifact-bucket-12345
Step 3️⃣: Create CodePipeline
Go to AWS Console → CodePipeline → Create pipeline

Source stage:

Choose GitHub (version 2)

Connect your GitHub repo where infra.yaml is stored

Build stage (optional for infra) → Skip (we don’t need build for infra templates)

Deploy stage:

Choose AWS CloudFormation

Action mode: Create or update stack

Stack name: devops-demo-stack

Template file: infra.yaml from source

Step 4️⃣: Test the Pipeline
Make a small change in infra.yaml (e.g., change EC2 name tag) and push it to GitHub.

CodePipeline automatically triggers.

CloudFormation stack updates → creates/updates S3 + EC2.

🔹 Real-Time Benefit
No need to log into AWS Console and click manually.

Every change in GitHub automatically updates infrastructure.

Same approach can be extended for:

VPC creation

IAM roles

RDS databases

Load balancers

🔹 Simple Workflow Recap
Developer pushes template → GitHub

CodePipeline detects change

Pipeline runs → CloudFormation executes

Infra is provisioned automatically

