Good question 👍
You saw the word stack when I explained CloudFormation + CodePipeline integration. Let me break it down very simply:

🔹 What is a Stack in CloudFormation?
A stack is simply a collection of AWS resources that CloudFormation manages together as a single unit.

Think of it like a container for your infrastructure.

Example:

If your infra.yaml template says → “create 1 EC2 + 1 S3 bucket”

When you deploy the template, CloudFormation will create both resources and keep them grouped under one stack.

So:

Stack Name = A label you give when deploying (devops-demo-stack for example).

Stack Resources = Whatever is inside the template (EC2, S3, RDS, etc.).

🔹 Real-Life Analogy
Imagine you are a DevOps engineer setting up a small office:

You need chairs, desks, and laptops.

You prepare a shopping list (CloudFormation template).

When you place the order → the shop delivers all the items together. That delivery is your stack.

If later you update your shopping list (e.g., add a printer), the shop updates your delivery. That’s stack update.

🔹 Example in AWS
If your template looks like:

Resources:
  MyBucket:
    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket
  MyEC2:
    Type: AWS::EC2::Instance
When you deploy it:

CloudFormation creates 1 stack (e.g., DevOpsStack)

Inside that stack → it manages 1 S3 bucket + 1 EC2 instance

If you delete the stack → both S3 bucket and EC2 instance are deleted automatically.

✅ Key Point:
A stack = one instance of your CloudFormation template deployed in AWS.

