what is difference between Codepipeline ,CodeBuild, CodeDeploy in AWS please explain for devops enginner with simple real time example on it
ChatGPT said:
Perfect 👍 This is one of the most common DevOps interview questions and also very important when working in real projects.
Let’s go step by step and compare CodePipeline, CodeBuild, and CodeDeploy with a real-time example.

🔹 1. AWS CodePipeline
👉 What it is:

A CI/CD orchestration service.

Think of it as a pipeline manager that connects all stages (Source → Build → Test → Deploy).

It doesn’t build or deploy by itself, but it calls CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, or other tools.

👉 Real-Life Analogy:
Imagine a factory conveyor belt.

The belt doesn’t build products, it just moves items through different stages.

Each station (cutting, painting, packaging) = build, test, deploy steps.

👉 Example in AWS:

You push code to GitHub.

CodePipeline detects it → sends it to CodeBuild → then CodeDeploy → then marks pipeline complete.

🔹 2. AWS CodeBuild
👉 What it is:

A build service (like Jenkins build job).

It compiles your source code, runs tests, and produces artifacts (JAR, Docker image, etc.).

👉 Real-Life Analogy:
Factory workers who actually assemble the product.

Example: taking raw wood and cutting it into chairs.

👉 Example in AWS:

You push Java Spring Boot code → CodeBuild compiles Maven project → produces a .jar file → stores it in S3 or ECR.

🔹 3. AWS CodeDeploy
👉 What it is:

A deployment service.

It takes built artifacts (from CodeBuild or S3) and deploys them to target environments (EC2, Lambda, ECS, On-prem servers).

👉 Real-Life Analogy:
The delivery team that installs the final chairs in your office.

👉 Example in AWS:

CodeDeploy takes your .jar file → copies it to EC2 → restarts Tomcat → application is live.

🔹 Simple Real-Time End-to-End Example
Let’s say you are a DevOps engineer for an e-commerce web app.

Developer pushes code to GitHub (new feature: add “wishlist” button).

CodePipeline (Manager) notices the new code and starts the process.

CodeBuild (Builder) compiles the code, runs unit tests, creates app.jar.

CodeDeploy (Delivery Team) takes app.jar and deploys it to:

EC2 instances (app servers), or

ECS containers, or

Lambda function (if serverless).

Pipeline completes → website shows new “wishlist” feature automatically.

🔹 Summary Table
Service	Role in CI/CD	Real-Life Role	Example in AWS
CodePipeline	Orchestrator	Conveyor belt manager	Detects GitHub commit, starts pipeline
CodeBuild	Builder	Factory worker	Builds .jar using Maven
CodeDeploy	Deployer	Delivery team	Deploys .jar to EC2/ECS/Lambda
✅ This is how companies automate continuous integration and deployment using these services.

